# KNOWN GAP — a closure that captures a COLLECTION-ELEMENT BORROW and returns it
# yields a raw ADDRESS instead of the element's value.
#
# `gg check` is clean and both backends exit 0; the program prints something like
# `187651284390560` where `42` is correct.
#
# AXIS CELL: capture source = `.get(i).unwrap()` on a local `Vector[int]`;
# capture form = an UNANNOTATED closure (`auto f = (): ...`). The ANNOTATED form
# `Callable[Ref[int]()] f = (): v.get(0).unwrap()` prints the correct `42` on both
# backends, so the two forms DISAGREE with each other — which is the sharper
# statement of the defect, and why this fixture pins the unannotated one.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS NOT THE CONTAINER-LITERAL MINT, and it is not fixed by it. Measured
# against the compiler both BEFORE and AFTER that fix: the address is printed
# either way. The literal mints a slot from an unmaterialized operand; this is a
# CLOSURE CAPTURE that never materializes the captured borrow at all, so the
# closure environment stores the pointer and the call returns it.
#
# Closure capture IS a documented ownership boundary — `docs/devbook/11` lists it
# among the positions where the destination must own — so a captured borrow owes
# the same materialization every other consuming position performs.
#
# INTENDED: prints `42` on both backends.
#
# Wired test: known_gap_closure_capture_collection_elem_borrow (tests/integration.rs,
# #[ignore]d).

void main():
    Vector[int] v = [42, 7]
    auto f = (): v.get(0).unwrap()
    print(f())
